// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_PISTIS_SOPHIA: Record<string, string> = {
  "gnostic.ps.1": "After rising from the dead, Jesus spent eleven years teaching his disciples, but only as far as the regions of the First Commandment and the First Mystery, the twenty-fourth mystery 'within the Veil'. He told them he had come from that mystery, and they took it to be the head and completion of everything. He had not yet explained the full layout of the higher worlds: the great Invisible and its powers and aeons, the Treasury of the Light with its saviours and guards, the Twin-saviour, the Amens, the five Trees, the seven Voices, the five Helpers, the great Light and the five Impressions. He had spoken of these only in general, so the disciples thought there was nothing beyond that mystery.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the Father in the form of a dove' may describe the First Mystery itself, or be a separate item in the list; the sentence is loosely joined",
  "gnostic.ps.2": "The disciples were sitting together on the Mount of Olives, rejoicing that they had received the whole truth, while Jesus sat a little apart. On the fifteenth day of the month Tybi, the day of the full moon, at sunrise, a great power of light came down from the highest mystery and surrounded Jesus completely. The light was so bright that the disciples could not see him, only many rays of light of different kinds, stretching from under the earth up to heaven. They were seized with great fear.",
  "gnostic.ps.3": "Wrapped in that light, Jesus rose into the sky while the disciples watched in silence. Three hours after he reached heaven, all the powers of the heavens were thrown into turmoil, and the whole earth shook, so that people thought the world was about to be rolled up. The commotion lasted until the ninth hour of the next day, and all the angels and powers sang praises to 'the innermost of the innermost' so loudly that the whole world heard them.",
  "gnostic.ps.4": "The disciples sat together weeping in fear, wondering whether the Saviour would destroy everything. At the ninth hour of the next day the heavens opened and they saw Jesus come down, shining even more brightly than when he had gone up, with a light no one could describe. The light was of three kinds, one above another, each brighter than the one below; the lowest was like the light that had first come over him before he ascended.",
  "gnostic.ps.5": "When Jesus saw how frightened the disciples were, he spoke to them kindly: 'Take courage. It is I, do not be afraid.'",
  "gnostic.ps.6": "The disciples asked him to draw his light back into himself, because they could not bear it. He did, and they came to him, bowed down and asked where he had been and why the earth had shaken. Jesus told them to rejoice: he had gone back to the regions he came from, and from now on he would speak openly, without parables, from the beginning of the Truth to its end. He explained that while he sat apart on the Mount of Olives he had been thinking that his mission was complete but that the highest mystery had not yet sent him his Vesture, which he had left behind there.",
  "gnostic.ps.7": "Jesus says that at sunrise, by command of the First Mystery, his Vesture of light was sent to him, so that he could begin to reveal the whole truth. He tells the disciples that when he came into the world he brought twelve powers from the twelve saviours of the Treasury of the Light and put them into their mothers' wombs; their souls therefore come from above, not from the rulers. Passing the rulers in the form of the angel Gabriel, he planted a power from 'the little Iao' in Elizabeth before she conceived John the Baptist, and had the soul of the prophet Elijah placed in John too. That is why he had said that John was Elijah who was to come.",
  "gnostic.ps.8": "Jesus goes on: he found Mary, his mother in the body, spoke to her in the form of Gabriel, and placed in her a power from Barbelo, which became his body, and a power from 'the great Sabaoth, the Good' in place of a soul. The twelve powers for the disciples he put into the sphere of the rulers, and the rulers' servants, thinking them ordinary souls, bound them into the disciples' mothers. He explains how a share of his power passed down through the First Commandment, the great Light and the five Helpers into the mixed world. He tells them to rejoice, for the time has come for him to put on his Vesture, and through them the world will be saved.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the body which I have borne in the height' may mean his heavenly body, or the body he bore after coming down; Mead's English allows both",
  "gnostic.ps.9": "Jesus says he has now put on his Vesture and received all authority through the First Mystery. Soon he will reveal the mystery and the fullness of the universe and hide nothing from them, making them perfect in all mysteries. First, he will tell them everything that happened to him.",
  "gnostic.ps.10": "Jesus describes the Vesture that came down to him at sunrise. On it was a mystery written in five words of the height, which he quotes and interprets: the powers above call him to come to them, for they are his own members, one with him; he is the First Mystery, which existed in the Ineffable before it came forth. They have sent him this vesture and will clothe him with two more. The first holds the glory of all the names of the spaces of the Ineffable; the second the glory of the names of the two spaces of the First Mystery; the third, the one just sent, the glory of the names of everything below, from the First Commandment and the Treasury of the Light down to the great Invisible, the twelve aeons, the Fate and the sphere. He is to put it on and come to them quickly, for soon he will leave the world.",
  "gnostic.ps.11": "Jesus says he put on the Vesture, shone exceedingly and rose up. When he reached the gate of the firmament its gates shook and opened. All the rulers, powers and angels there were thrown into confusion by the light; they saw the mystery of their own names on his vesture, their bonds came loose, and they fell down and worshipped, asking how the lord of the universe had passed through them without their knowing. They could not see him, only the light.",
  "gnostic.ps.12": "Jesus rose to the first sphere, shining forty-nine times more brightly than in the firmament. Its gates opened of themselves, and the rulers there, seeing the light and the mystery of their names on his vesture, were thrown into fear, lost their bonds and their places, and fell down to worship him, asking how the lord of the universe had passed through them unnoticed.",
  "gnostic.ps.13": "Jesus came to the second sphere, which is the Fate, shining forty-nine times more brightly than in the first sphere. Its gates opened by themselves, and its rulers, terrified, saw the mystery of their names on his vesture, lost their bonds and fell down to worship him with the same question: how had the lord of the universe passed through them without their knowing?",
  "gnostic.ps.14": "Jesus rose to the twelve aeons of the rulers. Their veils drew apart and their gates opened, and he entered shining forty-nine times more brightly than in the Fate. All their powers, from angels to the great invisible Forefather and the three triple-powers, were thrown into panic and fled, unable to close their regions. He explains that the light he had on earth was far weaker, because the world could not bear the true light without being destroyed; in the aeons it was 8,700 myriad (87 million) times greater.",
  "gnostic.ps.15": "All the powers in the twelve aeons were thrown into confusion, not knowing what was happening. Adamas, the great Tyrant, and the other tyrants fought in vain against the light, and were struck down, lifeless. Jesus took from them a third of their power, so that the sorceries the fallen angels taught humans would no longer work through them. He also changed the motion of the Fate and the sphere: where Yew, the Overseer of the Light, had set them always facing left, they now spend six months facing left and six months facing right.",
  "gnostic.ps.16": "Jesus sums up: when he came into the rulers' region they rebelled and fought the light; he took a third of their power so they could not do their evil works, and he changed the Fate and the sphere so that they face left for six months and right for six months.",
  "gnostic.ps.17": "Jesus says, 'Who has ears to hear, let him hear.' Mary (Magdalene) gazes into the air for an hour and asks permission to speak openly. Jesus calls her blessed, promises to perfect her in all the mysteries of the height, and tells her to speak, since her heart is raised to the kingdom of heaven more than any of her brothers'.",
  "gnostic.ps.18": "Mary explains Jesus's words. By taking a third of the rulers' power and changing their spheres, he has made sure that those who practise the sorcery the fallen angels taught, the astrologers and the diviners, can no longer foretell what will happen. She says the prophet Isaiah foresaw this in his 'vision about Egypt': 'Where now, Egypt, are your diviners and astrologers? Let them tell you what the Lord Sabaoth will do.'",
  "gnostic.ps.19": "Mary goes on: the power in Isaiah foretold that Jesus would take away the rulers' power and change their sphere and their Fate, so that from now on they would know nothing of what he does; the rulers are 'Egypt' because they are matter. The light-power Jesus received from Sabaoth, the Good, is in his body, and he said 'Who has ears to hear, let him hear' to find out whose heart is truly raised towards the kingdom of heaven.",
  "gnostic.ps.20": "Jesus praises Mary: she is blessed among all women and will be the fullness of all fullnesses. She asks whether people who know the magic of the rulers, taught by the fallen angels, will still be able to make it work. Jesus says not as before, since he has taken a third of the rulers' power; but they can 'borrow' from those who know the magic of the thirteenth aeon, whose power he has not taken away, and that magic will still work.",
  "gnostic.ps.21": "Mary asks whether astrologers and diviners will still be able to tell people what will happen to them. Jesus says that if they find the Fate and the sphere turned to the left, as they were first set, their predictions will come true; if turned to the right, they will say nothing true. He has changed the rulers' aspects (squares, triangles, octagons), and their names differ in each position, so anyone who does not know the new six-month pattern will be confused. He did this so that the rulers themselves would be confused and not understand their own paths.",
  "gnostic.ps.22": "Philip, who was writing down everything Jesus said, comes forward, bows down and asks permission to put a question. Jesus grants it. Philip asks why Jesus changed the binding and paths of the rulers, their Fate and their sphere: was it for the salvation of the world?",
  "gnostic.ps.23": "Jesus answers that he changed their path to save souls. Otherwise many souls would have been lost, and the number of perfect souls who will enter the Inheritance of the Height and the Treasury of the Light would have been long delayed. Thrown into confusion, the rulers give up the power in their matter from which they fashion souls, so that those who will be saved are purified and raised quickly, and those who will not be saved are quickly destroyed.",
  "gnostic.ps.24": "Mary, 'fair in her speech and blessed', asks Jesus not to be angry if she questions him so often. He tells her to speak, and she asks how souls would have been delayed outside, and how they will now be quickly purified.",
  "gnostic.ps.25": "Jesus praises Mary's question. Before he came, the rulers were bound in their spheres as Yew had set them. At fixed times Melchisedec, the great Receiver of the Light, came among them, stirred them up, and drew out the light in them, from their breath, tears and sweat, purifying it and taking it to the Treasury of the Light. Their leftover matter was gathered by the rulers' servants and shaped into the souls of humans, animals, reptiles and birds, which were sent down into the world. The receivers of the sun and moon also gathered light from the rulers' paths and handed it on to Melchisedec.",
  "gnostic.ps.26": "As the rulers lost their power, their light and their kingdom began to fail. So when Melchisedec came again and forced them to give up their light, they turned to their own leftover matter and devoured it, instead of letting it become souls in the world, so as not to be exhausted and lose their kingdom. In this way they hoped to delay the end until the number of perfect souls is complete.",
  "gnostic.ps.27": "For two cycles the rulers kept devouring their refuse so that no souls were born and their rule would last. When Jesus rose in his vesture, Adamas and the tyrants of the twelve aeons fought against its light to hold it among them. So, by command of the First Mystery, he changed their courses: six months facing their old aspects on the left, six months facing the right. Confused, they could no longer devour their refuse. He also shortened their cycles and times so that the number of perfect souls who receive the mysteries would be completed quickly. That, he says, is what he meant by 'I have shortened the times for the sake of my elect'; otherwise no soul would be saved. The disciples bow down and call themselves blessed.",
  "gnostic.ps.28": "Jesus describes how all the rulers of the twelve aeons, seeing the mystery of their names on his vesture, fell down, worshipped and asked how the lord of the universe had passed through them unnoticed. Their great ones, the triple-powers and forefathers, saw that the tyrants' power was reduced and were terrified; they could not come near because of the light and worshipped from a distance. The tyrants fell as if dead. When he left, all the aeons were bound back into their order, as he had set them, six months facing left and six months facing right; so too those in the Fate and the sphere.",
  "gnostic.ps.29": "Jesus rose to the thirteenth aeon, whose veils opened for him, and found Pistis Sophia below it, alone, grieving because she had not been let into the thirteenth aeon, her higher home, and because of the torments inflicted by Self-willed, one of the three triple-powers. She saw the mystery of her name on his vesture. Once, in the thirteenth aeon, she used to sing praises to the higher light she had seen in the veil of the Treasury of the Light, and the other emanations, twenty-four in all with her and her partner, gazed at that light.",
  "gnostic.ps.30": "Mary asks how Pistis Sophia, one of the twenty-four emanations, came to be below the thirteenth aeon. Jesus begins 'the story of Pistis Sophia'. In the thirteenth aeon she looked up, saw the light of the veil of the Treasury of the Light, longed to reach it, and stopped performing the mystery of her aeon, singing praises to that light instead. The rulers of the twelve aeons hated her for it, and so did the great triple-powered Self-willed, who had disobeyed by keeping back his light because he wanted to rule the thirteenth aeon. He joined the rulers, sent out from his matter a great lion-faced power and other violent emanations into the chaos to lie in wait for her, and pursued her so that she would look down, see that lion-faced light and long for it.",
  "gnostic.ps.31": "Sophia looked down and saw the lion-faced light. She did not know it belonged to Self-willed; she took it for the light she had seen above, and decided to go down, without her partner, take it, and make herself light-aeons with it to reach the Light of lights. She left the thirteenth aeon, passed down through the twelve aeons, whose rulers pursued her in anger, and went into the chaos. There the emanations of Self-willed surrounded her, and the lion-faced power devoured her light-powers. Her matter, thrust into the chaos, became a lion-faced ruler, half fire and half darkness: Yaldabaoth. Exhausted, she was hard pressed on every side.",
  "gnostic.ps.32.1": "Pistis Sophia cries to the Light of lights in which she had trusted, and sings her first repentance. She confesses that she looked down, saw a light, went to take it and found herself in the darkness of the chaos, unable to return, while the lion-faced power took her light. She cried for help, but her voice did not reach out of the darkness. The rulers of the aeons rejoiced over her though she had done them no harm. She says she acted in innocence, thinking that light belonged to the Light; for its sake she has become a stranger to her brothers, the invisibles, and is mocked by all who keep their mystery. She begs the Light, in its great mercy, to save her from the matter of this darkness and the emanations of Self-willed.",
  "gnostic.ps.32.2": "The first repentance goes on. Sophia asks the Light not to turn away, but to save her from the rulers who hate her. She waited for her partner to come and fight for her, and he did not come; when she sought light they gave her darkness. She asks that the darkness they brought on her become a snare for them, that they never see the light or return to the region of Self-willed, their impious god, who did not know that he could harm her only because the Light allowed it. She ends by praising the Light's name: let all 'matters' rejoice and seek the Light, for it will purify them, and a city will be prepared in the Light for the souls who are saved and who receive the mysteries.",
  "gnostic.ps.33": "Jesus says this was Sophia's first repentance, and asks who has ears to hear. Mary says her inner light has ears, and explains that the Light's power foretold this repentance through David in Psalm 68 (Psalm 69 in most Bibles), which she quotes in full: 'Save me, O God, for the waters are come in even unto my soul', the singer sunk in the mire, hated without cause, a stranger to his brothers, given gall and vinegar, praying that his enemies be trapped and blotted out of the book of the living, and ending in praise: God will save Zion, and those who love his name will dwell there. Line by line it matches Sophia's words.",
  "gnostic.ps.34": "Mary says that this is the solution of the mystery of Sophia's repentance. Jesus praises her: 'Well said, Mary, blessed one, the fullness', whom all generations will call blessed.",
  "gnostic.ps.35": "Jesus recites Sophia's second repentance: she asks the Light of lights, in which she has trusted from the beginning, not to leave her in the darkness, but to save her through its mysteries from the lion-faced power and the emanations of Self-willed. The rulers mocked her, saying 'She has ceased her mystery'; the emanations have taken her light and set a watch on her, saying 'The Light has forsaken her, let us seize her'. She prays that those who would take her power may fall and be wrapped in darkness.",
  "gnostic.ps.36": "Jesus asks if they understand. Peter breaks in: 'My Lord, we will not put up with this woman; she takes every chance from us and lets none of us speak.' Jesus says that whoever is moved by the power of his spirit to understand may come forward, and invites Peter to give the meaning of the second repentance. Peter quotes Psalm 70 (Psalm 71 in most Bibles): 'O God, I have trusted in thee, let me never be put to shame', a prayer to be rescued from the wicked, who say 'God has forsaken him; pursue and seize him', and that those who slander the singer be put to shame.",
  "gnostic.ps.37": "Jesus praises Peter and promises the disciples that he will perfect them in every mystery, make them 'spiritual', and give them all the mysteries of his Father, so that whomever they admit on earth will be admitted into the Light above, and whomever they expel will be expelled from the kingdom. Then he recites Sophia's third repentance: she asks that those who would take her light be put to shame and turned to darkness, that those who seek the Light rejoice, and that the Light, her saviour, save her quickly from the chaos.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'admit' and 'expel' may refer to admitting people to the mysteries or to forgiving and retaining sins; Mead's words allow both",
  "gnostic.ps.38": "Jesus asks whoever has a perceptive spirit to explain the third repentance. Martha comes forward, falls at his feet weeping, and asks to speak. Jesus takes her hand: 'Blessed is everyone who humbles himself.' Martha quotes Psalm 69 (Psalm 70 in most Bibles): 'O Lord God, give heed to my help'; let those who seek my soul be put to shame and those who say 'Ha, ha' turn back; let all who seek you rejoice; I am poor and needy, Lord, do not delay.",
  "gnostic.ps.39": "Jesus praises Martha and recites Sophia's fourth repentance, spoken before she was oppressed again. She asks the Light to hear her: her time has vanished like breath, she has become matter, her light has been taken and she has forgotten her mystery. She has become like a demon or a lonely decan in the air without light. Her partner said she had been filled with chaos. All this happened by the Light's command, but now its time is fulfilled: let the Light seek her power and soul, for the number is complete, and all rulers will fear its light. The Light looks down from the height to hear the sighing of those in chains and set free the bound power of souls.",
  "gnostic.ps.40": "John asks permission to explain the fourth repentance, and Jesus grants it. John quotes Psalm 101 (Psalm 102 in most Bibles): a prayer of one whose days vanish like smoke and whose bones are burned, who is like a pelican in the desert and a sparrow alone on the roof, eating ashes for bread; but the Lord endures for ever and will have mercy on Zion; he looks down from heaven to hear the groaning of the prisoners and set free those condemned to death.",
  "gnostic.ps.41": "Jesus calls John 'the Virgin, who shall rule in the kingdom of the Light'. He goes on: the emanations of Self-willed again pressed Sophia, wanting all her light, and the command to lead her out of the chaos had not yet come. She sang her fifth repentance: her power is full of darkness, she has become like the rulers of the chaos and like matter without power; the Light's spirit has left her and her fellow-emanations hate her. She asks whether the Light will not act in the chaos, and whether its deliverers will not come to proclaim its name there. Her pain began when she looked down at the light in the chaos; terrors have surrounded her like water, and neither her fellows nor her partner have been allowed to help.",
  "gnostic.ps.42": "Jesus asks whoever has ears, and whose spirit stirs, to explain the fifth repentance. Philip puts down the book in which he writes everything Jesus says and does, and complains that as the scribe he has never been able to come forward and speak, though his spirit has often urged him. Jesus answers that he, Thomas and Matthew have been charged by the First Mystery to write down everything Jesus says and does and everything they see, so as to bear witness to the kingdom of heaven. When Philip's share of the writing is done, he may come forward and speak.",
  "gnostic.ps.43": "Mary explains Jesus's words to Philip: through Moses it was foretold that 'by two or three witnesses every matter shall be established'; the three witnesses are Philip, Thomas and Matthew. Jesus approves, and tells Philip to give the meaning of the fifth repentance now and then return to writing. Philip quotes Psalm 87 (Psalm 88 in most Bibles): 'Lord, God of my salvation, by day and by night have I cried unto thee'; the singer's soul is full of troubles, he is counted among those gone down to the pit, his friends are taken from him, and he asks whether the dead will praise God.",
  "gnostic.ps.44": "Jesus praises Philip and sends him back to his writing. Then he recites Sophia's sixth repentance, sung when the Light forgave her for leaving her region and going down into the darkness: she has praised the Light in the darkness below; if the Light counted her sin she could not stand, but it is her saviour and has compassion, and its great saving mystery will rescue all the powers from the chaos. 'Whoever's mind is raised up, let him understand.'",
  "gnostic.ps.45": "Andrew explains the sixth repentance with Psalm 129 (Psalm 130 in most Bibles): 'Out of the depths have I cried unto thee, O Lord'; if the Lord marked iniquities, who could stand? But with him is forgiveness and great redemption. Jesus praises Andrew and promises to perfect the disciples in every mystery, from the Ineffable down to the lowest matter, and that they will be with him in his Father's kingdom. When the perfect number is complete and the 'Mixture' (the mixed world) is dissolved, the tyrant gods who have kept back their light will be thrown into the 'wise fire' until they give it up. Mary explains this from Psalm 81: 'God shall sit in the assembly of the gods and judge the gods.'",
  "gnostic.ps.46.1": "After the sixth repentance Sophia looked up to see whether her sins were forgiven and whether she would be led out of the chaos; but the First Mystery had not yet given the command, and she saw the rulers of the twelve aeons mocking her. Grieving, she sang her seventh repentance: she has lifted her power to the Light and trusted it, and asks not to be scorned, to be shown the paths out of the chaos, and to be forgiven the sin she committed in ignorance through the Light's great mystery of forgiveness. The Light gives every one who trusts in it the mystery that suits him, and an inheritance in the Treasury of the Light. She asks it to save her from the rulers who hate her out of jealousy.",
  "gnostic.ps.46.2": "Sophia ends her seventh repentance asking to be saved from the emanations of Self-willed. Thomas says he has held back so as not to annoy his brothers, and gives the meaning from Psalm 24 (Psalm 25 in most Bibles): 'Unto thee, O Lord, have I lifted up my soul'; show me your ways, remember not the sins of my youth, forgive my sin, for it is great; look on my affliction and on my enemies, and deliver Israel. Jesus praises Thomas: all generations will bless the disciples, who have received his spirit and become 'spiritual'; soon he will fill them with all light and power and speak to them of the height.",
  "gnostic.ps.47": "After the seventh repentance the command to save Sophia had still not come, but Jesus, out of compassion and without command, led her to a roomier place in the chaos. The emanations of Self-willed eased off, thinking she was being led out entirely. Sophia did not recognise him; she went on praising the Light of the Treasury, thinking it was her helper. When the emanations saw she had not been led out, they turned on her again, and she sang her eighth repentance: she has hoped in the Light and asks to be saved from the lion-faced power, into whose hands she has not been left; the Light has led her to a place where she is not oppressed.",
  "gnostic.ps.48": "The lion-faced power and the emanations of Self-willed, seeing she had not been fully freed, attacked Sophia again, and she continued the same repentance: her light and understanding are shaken, her power fades; she is weak before the rulers and the twenty-four emanations, and her partner was afraid to help her. All the rulers count her as matter without light and say she has become chaos. But she trusts the Light and asks it to send its light over her and to silence those who would devour her with guile.",
  "gnostic.ps.49": "Matthew says the Spirit has stirred him, and explains the eighth repentance from Psalm 30 (Psalm 31 in most Bibles): 'In thee, O Lord, have I hoped; let me never be put to shame'; draw me out of the net they have hidden for me; into your hands I commit my spirit; I have become a reproach to my enemies and forgotten like a corpse, but my times are in your hands; let the lying lips be silenced.",
  "gnostic.ps.50": "Jesus praises Matthew: when the perfect number is complete he will sit in the Treasury of the Light and the disciples on twelve light-powers, until the orders of the twelve saviours are restored to their places. Mary recalls his earlier saying that they would sit on twelve thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel. Jesus goes on to Sophia's ninth repentance: she asks the Light to strike down those who took her power, to let Yew, its angel, pursue them into the darkness, and to take the light from the lion-faced power that was set as a trap for her. They pressed her to tell the mystery of the Light, which she did not know, and mocked her; she asks to be saved so that she may return to the thirteenth aeon and sing the Light's praise there.",
  "gnostic.ps.51": "James kisses Jesus's breast and explains the ninth repentance from Psalm 34 (Psalm 35 in most Bibles): 'Plead my cause, O Lord, with them that strive with me'; let my persecutors be like chaff before the wind, chased by the angel of the Lord; false witnesses ask me things I do not know and repay me evil for good; do not let my enemies gloat, saying 'We have swallowed him up'; my tongue will speak of your righteousness all day long.",
  "gnostic.ps.52": "Jesus praises James: the disciples, and all who perform his mysteries, will be first in the kingdom, before all the invisibles, gods and rulers. Mary explains this with 'The last shall be first': the invisibles and rulers were created first, but humans who receive the mysteries will enter first. Then, when the lion-faced power attacked her again, Sophia cried out once more to the Light, and her repentance was accepted. Sent by the First Mystery, Jesus came down secretly through the midst of the aeons, without any power knowing, to lead her out. She saw him shining ten thousand times more brightly than the lion-faced power, knew he came from the Height, took courage and sang her tenth repentance: the Light has heard her; she was far from her dwelling, among the merciless, who fought her without cause.",
  "gnostic.ps.53": "Peter explains the tenth repentance from Psalm 119 (Psalm 120 in most Bibles): 'In my distress I cried unto the Lord, and he heard me'; save me from lying lips and a deceitful tongue; woe is me that I dwell in the tents of Kedar; I am for peace, but when I speak they are for war. Jesus praises him.",
  "gnostic.ps.54": "When the lion-faced power saw Jesus coming near Sophia, it grew still more furious and sent out a host of violent emanations. Sophia sang her eleventh repentance: why has the mighty power raised itself in evil? She chose to go down into the chaos rather than stay in the thirteenth aeon, and they tried to trick her out of her light; but the Light will take theirs away, and the twenty-four emanations will see what has happened to the lion-faced power and learn obedience. Salome explains it from Psalm 51 (Psalm 52 in most Bibles): 'Why boastest thou thyself in mischief, O mighty man?' God will uproot him; but I am like a green olive tree in the house of God. Jesus praises Salome.",
  "gnostic.ps.55": "As Jesus came near the chaos to take away the lion-faced power's light, it cried in fear to its god, Self-willed, who looked down from the thirteenth aeon in great anger; the lion-faced power and its emanations surrounded Sophia again. She saw Self-willed's anger, was afraid, and sang her twelfth repentance against him and his emanations: they repaid her praise with hatred. She prays that darkness come over Self-willed, that his power be given to another of the three triple-powers, that the Purifier take his lights, that his name be blotted out of the thirteenth aeon, and that he wrap himself in the chaos like a garment for ever. She asks for mercy, for she has become like fallen matter, tossed about like a demon in the air.",
  "gnostic.ps.56": "Andrew explains the twelfth repentance from Psalm 108 (Psalm 109 in most Bibles): 'Hold not thy peace, O God of my praise'; the wicked have spoken against me with lying tongues and repaid my love with hatred; let another take his office, let his children be fatherless and his wife a widow; he loved cursing, let it come to him, and let it clothe him like a garment; but help me, Lord, for I am poor and needy, my knees weak from fasting.",
  "gnostic.ps.57": "Sophia cries to the Light that she has sinned in the twelve aeons and sung twelve repentances, one for each, and asks forgiveness. Then she sings a thirteenth repentance for the thirteenth aeon, the region she came down from: she asks to be saved by the Light's great mystery, given baptism and purified; her sin is the lion-faced power, for whose sake she went down, and she transgressed so that the Light's command would be fulfilled. Martha explains it from Psalm 50 (Psalm 51 in most Bibles): 'Have mercy upon me, O God, according to thy great mercy; blot out my sin; wash me thoroughly from my iniquity.' Jesus praises her.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'I have transgressed, that thy commandment may be accomplished' may mean that her fall was itself part of the Light's plan, or only that it gave occasion for the Light's command to be carried out",
  "gnostic.ps.58": "When the time came for Sophia to be led out, Jesus, on his own and before the First Mystery's command, sent a light-power down into the chaos. It led her up to the higher regions of the chaos, though not yet out of it. The emanations of Self-willed chased after her, and she sang a song of praise: the Light has saved her and led her higher; may her pursuers sink into the lower darkness and fail to take her light, since the Light's power is with her, and she will not be afraid. Salome explains it from an Ode of Solomon: 'I will give thanks unto thee, O Lord'; let those who pursue me fall and not see me; their plans have come to nothing; my hope is in the Lord. Jesus praises her.",
  "gnostic.ps.59": "Jesus made the light-power he had sent into a wreath of light on Sophia's head, so that Self-willed's emanations could no longer master her. The evil matter in her was shaken off and left in the chaos, and she sang: 'The Light has become a wreath round my head ... though all my matters perish, I shall not perish, for the Light is with me.' Mary, Jesus's mother, asks to speak. Jesus says she received her form from Barbelo and her likeness from the Virgin of Light, and that his material body came from her. She explains the song from the nineteenth Ode of Solomon: 'The Lord is on my head as a wreath ... thou hast sprouted upon me.' Jesus blesses her: the pledge of the First Mystery dwells with her, and through it all will be saved.",
  "gnostic.ps.60": "After Sophia's thirteenth repentance, the time decreed by the First Mystery for her sufferings was over. The First Mystery sent a great light-power down from the height, and Jesus, who was above the chaos, sent out another from himself; the two met and became one great stream of light. Mary Magdalene explains this from Psalm 84 (Psalm 85 in most Bibles): 'Grace and truth met together, and righteousness and peace kissed each other; truth sprouted out of the earth, and righteousness looked down from heaven.' Grace and righteousness are the power from the First Mystery; truth and peace are the power that went out of Jesus, and 'peace' is the power that will take back Sophia's stolen lights and reconcile them with her.",
  "gnostic.ps.61": "Jesus praises Mary Magdalene as one who will inherit the whole Light-kingdom. Then Mary his mother asks to give the same verse's meaning. She tells a story: when Jesus was a child, working in a vineyard with Joseph, a spirit exactly like him came to her house asking for 'Jesus, my brother'. Thinking it a phantom sent to test her, she tied it to the bed and fetched Jesus and Joseph. The child Jesus was glad; when they came in, the spirit was untied, embraced and kissed him, and the two became one. She explains: 'grace' is the spirit sent from the height to forgive the world's sins; 'truth' is the power from Barbelo that became his body in her; 'righteousness' is his spirit that brought the mysteries; 'peace' is the power in his body that baptized people and reconciled them with his spirit.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'spirit' may be understood as Jesus's heavenly counterpart joining his earthly self, as the story suggests, or more loosely as the Holy Spirit coming upon him; the text says only that they 'became one'",
  "gnostic.ps.62": "The other Mary (Mary Magdalene) asks to give a further meaning. She relates the verse to Jesus's baptism by John: 'grace' is the spirit that came on him then, meeting the power of Sabaoth, the Good, within him; 'righteousness' is the spirit of the Light bringing the mysteries; 'peace' is Sabaoth's power that baptized and forgave; 'truth sprouting from the earth' is that power born of Mary his mother. Then his mother speaks once more: 'grace and truth met' is her meeting with Elizabeth, the mother of John; grace is the power in her, which is Jesus, and truth the power in Elizabeth, which is John. She adds that Jesus, in the form of Gabriel, looked down on her from heaven and spoke to her, and then 'sprouted up' in her. Jesus says these are the full meanings of David's words.",
  "gnostic.ps.scribe-note": "A note added by a later copyist from another book, not part of the dialogue. It gives secret names 'from the Boundless onward', to be written with a sign so that the Sons of God may be revealed: the name of the Immortal, the name of the Voice by which the Perfect Man was set in motion, and interpretations of each name. The names are strings of repeated vowels and letters, and several are unreadable in the copies used here.",
  "gnostic.ps.63": "John asks to explain the same verse. He recalls Jesus's own words: 'I came out of the Height, entered into Sabaoth, the Good, and embraced the light-power in him.' 'Grace' is Jesus, sent by his Father, the First Mystery, to have mercy on the world. 'Truth' is the power of Sabaoth, the Good, which the little Sabaoth cast into the matter of Barbelo; that matter is now Jesus's body. 'Righteousness' is Jesus bringing the mysteries to the rulers, who became good; 'peace' is the power of Sabaoth, his soul, which made the rulers of the six aeons of Yabraoth at peace with the Light. 'Righteousness looking down from heaven' is the First Mystery coming down upon the light-vesture, Jesus, as a dove. The First Mystery calls John 'beloved brother'.",
  "gnostic.ps.64": "The First Mystery (Jesus) goes on: the power from the Height (himself), the power that went out of him, and the soul he received from Sabaoth, the Good, came together as one shining light-stream. He called down the angels Gabriel and Michael and gave them the stream to take into the chaos. It shone through the whole chaos, terrified Self-willed's emanations, and drew out of them all the light-powers they had taken from Sophia. Gabriel and Michael poured these back into her, keeping nothing for themselves. Her body of matter shone again, her failing powers revived and recognised one another, and the stream left the chaos.",
  "gnostic.ps.65": "Peter explains this from an Ode of Solomon: 'A stream came forth and became a great wide flood'; nothing could hold it; it was led over the whole land; those on the dry sand drank; blessed are the ministers of that draught; they raised fallen limbs and gave light to eyes; they knew themselves in the Lord and were saved by the water of eternal life. He then explains each line: the flood is the light-stream; the temple it was drawn over is Jesus himself; the ministers are Gabriel and Michael, who took nothing for themselves; the revived limbs and souls are Sophia's powers and body, which got their light back and recognised one another. Jesus praises Peter.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Mead's text gives no explanation after 'They who were in the dry sand, drank'; the line may have been lost in the manuscript, or meant to be covered by the next explanation",
  "gnostic.ps.66.1": "Before Jesus had been commanded to lead Sophia out, Self-willed's emanations saw her shining again and cried to him for help. Self-willed sent another great light-power down from the thirteenth aeon like a flying arrow. His emanations took courage and attacked her in new forms: a great serpent, a seven-headed basilisk and a dragon, together with the lion-faced power. Adamas, the Tyrant of the twelve aeons, angry that she sought the Light above them all, sent down a demon power that threw her to the ground. Sophia cried to the Light to save her from them all, and at the First Mystery's command Jesus sent Gabriel, Michael and the light-stream again, ordering the angels to carry her so that her feet would not touch the darkness.",
  "gnostic.ps.66.2": "When the emanations saw the light-stream they fled in terror. The stream surrounded Sophia on every side and became a wreath round her head; she was no longer afraid of any of them. Those that came near fell to her left and right and could not harm her, because she had trusted in the Light. Then Jesus himself went down into the chaos, took the light of the lion-faced power, stripped all the emanations of their power and shut them in the chaos. He led Sophia out, with Gabriel and Michael at her side; she trod on the serpent, the seven-headed basilisk, the lion and the dragon. He made her stand on the basilisk, the worst of them, and destroyed its matter so that nothing would grow from it again.",
  "gnostic.ps.67.1": "James explains this from Psalm 90 (Psalm 91 in most Bibles): 'He that dwelleth under the help of the Most High ... thou wilt not be afraid of the terror by night, nor of the arrow that flieth by day ... a thousand will fall at thy left hand and ten thousand at thy right ... he will give his angels charge over thee, to bear thee up in their hands ... thou wilt tread on the serpent and basilisk, the lion and the dragon.' He begins to explain each line: dwelling under the Most High is Sophia sheltering in the light-stream; the shield is the stream around her; the 'night' is the chaos; the 'arrow that flieth by day' is the power Self-willed sent down from the thirteenth aeon, called 'day' because that aeon gives light to all the aeons.",
  "gnostic.ps.67.2": "James goes on: the thing slinking in darkness is the lion-faced power; the demon at mid-day is the demon power of Adamas, called 'mid-day' because the twelve aeons it came from lie midway between the thirteenth aeon and the chaos. The thousand falling on either side are the emanations that fell left and right of Sophia. Seeing the 'requital of the sinners' means she saw the First Mystery take their light and imprison them, repaying them as they had tried to rob her. The angels bearing her up are Gabriel and Michael keeping her feet from the darkness; treading on serpent, basilisk, lion and dragon is her trampling the emanations as she came out, saved because she trusted the Light.",
  "gnostic.ps.68": "Jesus praises James. Then he recites the song Sophia sang once she was out of the chaos: she is saved and freed from the bonds of darkness; the Light was all round her and kept Self-willed's emanations away. They had taken her power and thrown her into the chaos so that she became like heavy matter; then the light-stream came, shone on every side of her, cleared away her evil matter and raised her up, and her oppressors withdrew. Jesus asks who has ears to hear.",
  "gnostic.ps.69": "Thomas explains the song from an Ode of Solomon: 'I am saved from the bonds and am fled unto thee, O Lord'; you were at my right hand; I was despised and cast out, become as lead in their sight; you set lamps at my right and left so that no side of me was without light; I was relieved of the 'coats of skin'; your right hand raised me up. Line by line he pairs each phrase with Sophia's words: being 'as lead' is her becoming heavy matter; the lamps on either side are the light-stream surrounding her; the coats of skin are the evil matter she was purified of.",
  "gnostic.ps.70": "Jesus praises Thomas and recites Sophia's second song: by the Light's command she was led down from the higher aeon and then up out of the lower regions; the Light scattered Self-willed's emanations, gave her power to free herself from the bonds of Adamas's emanations, struck down the seven-headed basilisk with her hands and set her above its matter so that its seed would not rise again. It made straight her way out of the chaos, filled her lightless limbs with pure light from the Height, and placed the light of its stream in her, so that she has become pure light.",
  "gnostic.ps.71.1": "Matthew explains the second song from an Ode of Solomon: 'He who hath led me down out of the higher regions ... hath led me up out of the regions in the bottom below'; he scattered my foes and gave me power over the bonds; he struck the seven-headed serpent with my hands and set me above its root to destroy its seed; he freed them from the tombs, clothed dead bones with a body, and gave life to those who did not stir; he led his aeon above decay and made them a holy dwelling-place. He begins to pair each line with Sophia's words.",
  "gnostic.ps.71.2": "Matthew goes on: the seven-headed serpent is the basilisk Sophia was set above; the venom destroyed by the Lord's right hand is the power taken from Self-willed's emanations; the tombs and corpses are the chaos and its dark emanations, out of which she was freed; the dead bones clothed with a body are her lightless powers and limbs, filled with life-light from the Height; leading the aeon above decay is her being led up above the chaos; the riches piled on the holy dwelling-place are the light of the stream resting on her.",
  "gnostic.ps.72": "Jesus praises Matthew and recites more of Sophia's song: she declares the Light the highest, which saved her and led her up from the chaos. Mary wants to explain it but says she is afraid of Peter, 'because he threatened me and hateth our sex'. Jesus answers that no one can stop anyone filled with the spirit of light from coming forward to speak, and tells her to go on. She quotes Psalm 29 (Psalm 30 in most Bibles): 'I will exalt thee, O Lord, for thou hast received me ... thou hast led up my soul out of hell.'",
  "gnostic.ps.73": "Jesus praises Mary and gives two further pieces of Sophia's song: the Light has turned her darkness into light, torn apart the chaos around her and girded her with light. Martha matches the first with Psalm 29 (30): 'He hath changed my lamentation into joy; he hath rent my mourning-robe and girded me with joy.' The second piece calls on her power to praise the Light, which forgave her transgression, saved her from Self-willed's emanations, crowned her with light, and filled her with pure light, so that her beginning will be renewed as one of the invisibles of the Height.",
  "gnostic.ps.74": "Mary explains the last song from Psalm 102 (Psalm 103 in most Bibles): 'Bless the Lord, O my soul ... thy youth will renew itself as an eagle's'; the eagle lives on the heights, so Sophia will shine like the invisibles of the Height, as she did at first. Jesus then tells how he took Sophia up to a region below the thirteenth aeon, gave her a new mystery of the Light (not that of her own aeon) and a song of the Light, so that the rulers could no longer prevail against her, and left her there until he should come back for her. She sang that the Light had raised her from the chaos to a higher, firmer aeon. Andrew explains it from Psalm 39 (Psalm 40 in most Bibles): 'He hath set my feet on a rock ... and put a new song in my mouth.'",
  "gnostic.ps.75": "Jesus says these are all Sophia's adventures. As he was about to leave her and go to the Light, she feared that Adamas and Self-willed, with the lion-faced emanation, would come back and take all her light again, and asked him to take their light away first. He answered that his Father had not yet commanded that, but he would seal the regions of Self-willed and of Adamas and their rulers, so that none could fight her until their time was completed and his Father ordered him to take their light.",
  "gnostic.ps.76": "Jesus told Sophia that this would come when 'three times' were complete. She would know it when the gate of the Treasury of the Great Light, the left gate after the thirteenth aeon, was opened, for a great light would then fill every aeon. Until then they could do her no harm; she could go down into the twelve aeons and return to her place, but not pass through the gate into the thirteenth aeon. When the three times were complete, Adamas and Self-willed would attack again, blaming her for imprisoning their power in the chaos; she should then sing to the Light, and he would come quickly to take their light and bring her home. Jesus, sitting with the disciples on the Mount of Olives, adds that this time came while he was with them, before his vesture had been sent to him.",
  "gnostic.ps.77": "When the time came, Adamas looked down from the twelve aeons and saw that his demon power in the chaos was dark and could not return to him, because Jesus had taken its light. Blaming Sophia, he became furious and sent out two dark, violent emanations, made a dark region in his own region to imprison her, and set his rulers chasing her, intending to take her light and give it to his darkened power so that it could climb back out of the chaos. Sophia sang to the Light as she had been told: if she has wronged that power, let the rulers take her light; otherwise let the Light rise in anger against her foes and come quickly, as it promised.",
  "gnostic.ps.78": "James explains this song from Psalm 7: 'O Lord, my God, in thee have I hoped; free me from my pursuers ... lest he steal away my soul as a lion ... if I have done this, if there is injustice on my hands ... let the foe pursue my soul ... arise, O Lord, in thy wrath.' Jesus praises him.",
  "gnostic.ps.79": "Sophia turned to see whether Adamas and his rulers had gone back, and saw them still pursuing her. She told them: why do you say I shall have no help? The Light is my strong champion; it is patient, but if you do not turn back it will make its power ready and take your lights, so that you go down into darkness. Then, seeing the two dark emanations and the dark chaos Adamas had made for her, she was afraid and cried to the Light: Adamas has made a dark emanation and another chaos to throw me into; take from him his own light, and let his plan to take mine fall back on him.",
  "gnostic.ps.80": "Martha explains Sophia's words from Psalm 7: 'God is a righteous judge, strong and patient ... if ye turn not, he will whet his sword ... it hath digged a pit and will fall into the hole it hath made; its wrong will return on its own head.' The First Mystery praises her.",
  "gnostic.ps.81": "Having told the disciples all of Sophia's adventures in the chaos, Jesus says that afterwards he led her, shining beyond measure, into the thirteenth aeon. The twenty-four invisibles were thrown into commotion; they recognised Sophia but not him, taking him for some emanation of the Light-land. Overjoyed, Sophia stood among them and sang of what had been done for her: she had wandered in places without light, unable to return; she had transgressed by leaving her region; the Light heard her in her distress, broke her bonds, shattered the gates of darkness and the bolts of the chaos, and sent its stream to save her. She wanted them to know that Jesus had gone to the world of men and given them the mysteries of the Height.",
  "gnostic.ps.82": "Philip explains the song from Psalm 106 (Psalm 107 in most Bibles): 'Give thanks unto the Lord, for he is good'; the redeemed wandered in the desert, hungry and thirsty, sat in darkness bound in iron because they had rebelled; they cried to the Lord and he broke the gates of brass and the bars of iron and led them out. He pairs the first lines with Sophia's song and says 'and the rest of the Psalm'. Jesus praises him. Here, Mead marks, the story of Pistis Sophia ends.",
  "gnostic.ps.83": "Mary Magdalene asks Jesus not to be angry at her questions: he told them 'Seek and ye shall find, knock and it shall be opened', and no one in the world or the aeons but he can answer what they ask, since they question with the knowledge of the Height he gave them. Jesus tells her to ask freely: he rejoices when they question everything precisely. She asks what the twenty-four invisibles are like, of what kind they are, and what their light is like.",
  "gnostic.ps.84": "Jesus says nothing in this world can be compared with the twenty-four invisibles. Each is nine times greater than the heaven, the sphere and the twelve aeons together, and they shine ten thousand times more than the sun; yet the sun's true form, in the region of the Virgin of Light, is ten thousand times brighter than they are. Soon he will take the disciples through all the regions of the Height, except the space of the Ineffable, and they will see each region's glory directly. He describes an ascending ladder: the rulers of the Fate, the twelve aeons, the thirteenth aeon, the Midst, the Right, the Treasury of the Light, and the region of those who have received the mysteries. Seen from each higher level, the one below looks like darkness and shrinks to a speck of dust.",
  "gnostic.ps.85": "Mary Magdalene asks again not to anger him with her questions. Jesus promises to answer everything plainly and to perfect them in every knowledge, from the innermost Ineffable to the outermost darkness. Mary asks: if, as he said, the Treasury of the Light looks like a speck of dust from the region of those who have received the mysteries, will people of this world who receive the mysteries be higher than the emanations of the Treasury in his kingdom?",
  "gnostic.ps.86.1": "Jesus says this will happen only at the consummation of the aeon, the 'ascension of the universe'. Then the twelve saviours of the Treasury, with their orders (the emanations of the seven Voices and the five Trees), will reign with him in the inheritances of the Light, each over his own emanations and according to his rank. He lists them one by one: the saviour of the first Voice will be in the region of the souls who received the first mystery of the First Mystery, the second in the region of the second mystery, and so on through the seventh Voice and the five Trees to the twelfth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the consummation of the aeon' may mean the end of this world-age only, or the final end of all the lower worlds; Mead's phrasing allows both",
  "gnostic.ps.86.2": "Jesus continues the order of the kingdom: the seven Amens, the five Trees and the three Amens will be at his right; the Twin-saviours (the Child of the Child) and the nine guards at his left. He gives their ranking, and says that Yew, the guard of the veil, Melchisedec the great receiver of the Light, the two great guides and Sabaoth the Good (whom he calls his father) came from the light of the five Trees, and will reign with the first saviour. The Virgin of Light, the great guide of the Midst (whom the rulers call the Great Yew) and his twelve ministers, from whom the disciples got their shape and power, will also reign there. The fifteen helpers of the seven virgins of light and the angels of the Midst will spread into the regions of the twelve saviours, and Jesus will rule over all.",
  "gnostic.ps.86.3": "None of this happens yet; until the number of perfect souls is complete, every power stays in the region where it was set. Souls who receive the mysteries, and rulers who repent, will pass upwards through the Midst, where they are baptized, anointed and sealed, then through the Right, the nine guards, the Twin-saviours, the Amens, the saviours, the Trees and the Voices, each giving its seal, until they reach the place in the Inheritance that matches the mysteries they received. Human souls who receive the mysteries will go ahead of the repentant rulers and of all the powers of the Midst, the Right and the Treasury. Meanwhile those powers do their work of gathering and sealing the souls.",
  "gnostic.ps.87": "Mary Magdalene says she understands: because Jesus said human souls with the mysteries will enter the Light before the repentant rulers and the powers of the Right and the Treasury, he once told them 'The first will be last and the last first'; the 'last' are the human race, who will enter the kingdom ahead of the powers of the Height, the 'first'. And by 'Who has ears to hear, let him hear' he was testing whether they understood. Jesus is amazed at her answers, 'for she had become pure spirit utterly', and praises her.",
  "gnostic.ps.88": "Jesus says that if he took them to the region of the last Helper, who surrounds the Treasury of the Light, the whole Inheritance of the Light would look no bigger than a city. He will tell them about the Helper above that one too; but the regions above all the Helpers cannot be described, since nothing in this world, or even in the Height of Righteousness, is like them. Mary asks not to anger him by questioning so often: the disciples ask everything precisely so that they can preach it to all people, who may then repent, escape the cruel judgments of the evil rulers and the receivers of the outer darkness, and inherit the Light-kingdom. Jesus, moved with compassion, tells her to ask.",
  "gnostic.ps.89": "Mary asks how much greater, farther and brighter the second Helper is than the first. Jesus says that the second is immeasurably distant from the first in every direction, immeasurably greater and incomparably brighter, beyond the measure of angels, archangels, gods and invisibles. The same holds for the third, fourth and fifth Helpers, each beyond the one before. He will describe the form of each when he explains the unfolding of the universe.",
  "gnostic.ps.90": "Mary asks what those who have received the mystery of the Light will be like within the last Helper. Jesus says that when they leave the body made of the rulers' matter, each will stand in the order that matches the mystery he received, higher mysteries in higher orders and lower in lower: 'Where your heart is, there will your treasure be.' John asks to speak, and asks whether everyone must stay in his own region, with no power to go into the orders above or below him.",
  "gnostic.ps.91.1": "Jesus answers that each person stays in the region whose mysteries he has received and cannot rise into the orders above him, but he can go into all the orders below. He works up through the levels: the first Commandment, the First Mystery (the twenty-fourth mystery from without), the twenty-four mysteries, the First Mystery of the third space, and the first, second and third 'Thrice-spirituals', each with access to what lies beneath it but not above. Only one who receives the master-mystery of the First Mystery of the Ineffable (the twelve mysteries of the First Mystery together) can pass through every order in every direction and dwell where he pleases in the Inheritance.",
  "gnostic.ps.91.2": "Jesus says such a person will be king over all the orders of the Inheritance when the world is dissolved. Then he begins a long list of what 'that mystery of the Ineffable which I am' knows: why darkness and light arose; why the chaos and the Treasury; judgments and the Light-land; sinners and the inheritances; wrath and peace; cursing and blessing; adultery and purity; tears and laughter; strength and weakness; poverty and wealth; freedom and slavery; death and life. All pairs of opposites, and their reasons, are within its knowledge.",
  "gnostic.ps.92": "The disciples rejoice. Jesus continues the list of what the mystery of the Ineffable knows: why mercy and mercilessness arose; why ruin and eternity; why reptiles and wild beasts arose and why they will be destroyed; why cattle, birds, mountains and precious stones; gold, silver, copper, iron, lead, glass and wax; plants, waters, seas and the creatures in them; and why the matter of the world arose and why the world will be utterly destroyed.",
  "gnostic.ps.93": "Jesus tells the disciples to stay alert, for he will now speak of all the knowledge of the Ineffable. It knows why the four directions, demons and mankind, heat and cool air, stars and clouds, famine and plenty, frost, dew, hail, snow and the winds arose; why the stars, the firmament, the rulers of the spheres and aeons, the decans, angels, archangels, lords and gods arose; why jealousy and concord, hate and love, impiety and fear of God; and why every power of the Height arose, up to the twelve saviours, the gates of the Treasury, the nine guards, the Amens and the five Trees. Finally, it knows why the 'Mixture' arose and why it is being purified.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the Mixture which existeth not' may mean that the mixed world has no true existence, or that it will cease to exist when it is purified",
  "gnostic.ps.94": "Jesus tells the disciples to summon their power of perceiving the Light, for he will now speak of the region of the Ineffable itself. At this they lose heart completely. Mary Magdalene throws herself at his feet weeping: her brothers have given up, because he promised to speak of the whole knowledge of the Ineffable and did not finish. If all this knowledge is contained in that mystery, what person in the world could ever understand it?",
  "gnostic.ps.95.1": "Jesus comforts them: the mystery of the Ineffable belongs to them and to anyone who listens to them and renounces the world, its matter, its evil thoughts and its cares. For one who renounces the world and submits to the godhead, it is easier and quicker to understand than any other mystery; that is why he said 'Come unto me, all ye that are heavy laden ... my yoke is easy.' He goes on to describe what that mystery knows at a higher level: why the five Helpers, the great Light of lights, the first Commandment (divided into seven mysteries), the Great Light of the Impressions of the Light and the First Mystery 'rent themselves asunder' and came forth from 'the Fatherless'.",
  "gnostic.ps.95.2": "Jesus continues: that mystery knows why each of the higher orders of the space of the Ineffable divided itself and came forth from the Fatherless: the twelve Immovables, the Unwaverables, the Incomprehensibles, the Undesignatables, the Unindicatables, the Super-deeps, the Unspeakables, the Imperishables, the Impassables, the twelve Uncontainables, and the twenty-four myriads of Praise-singers outside the veil of the First Mystery, the 'twin-mystery' that looks both within and without.",
  "gnostic.ps.95.3": "Jesus finishes this list: the mystery knows why the Uncontainables of the second space divided and came forth; why the twenty-four mysteries of each of the three 'Thrice-spirituals' divided, and from which Thrice-spiritual each came forth; why the five Trees and the 'Fore-uncontainables' of each Thrice-spiritual came forth; why the third Thrice-spiritual came from the twelfth 'Pro-thrice-spiritual' in the last region of the Fatherless; why all the regions of the space of the Ineffable came forth from the 'last Limb' of the Ineffable; and it knows itself, and why it came forth from the Ineffable, which rules and arranges all.",
  "gnostic.ps.96.1": "Jesus says he will explain all these beings fully when he describes the 'expansion of the universe'. The mystery of the Ineffable is in all of them: their going-out, their going-up and their setting-up; it is the one and only word of the Ineffable and the knowledge of everything. Anyone who receives that one word, when the receivers come at death to free his soul from the body made of the rulers' matter, becomes a great stream of light among them; they are terrified, lose their power and fall down.",
  "gnostic.ps.96.2": "That soul soars upward as a stream of light. No power can seize or come near it; it passes through every region of the rulers and of the Light without giving the answers, defences or tokens other souls must give, while every region sings its praise in fear, until it reaches the inheritance of the mystery of the Ineffable and becomes one with its Limbs. The whole journey takes as long as it takes to shoot an arrow. Whoever receives and performs this mystery 'is a man in the world, but towers above' every rank of being: angels, archangels, tyrants, lords, gods, light-givers, triple-powers, forefathers, invisibles, the powers of the Midst and the Treasury, and the Mixture itself.",
  "gnostic.ps.96.3": "Such a person, Jesus goes on, rules with him and is king in the Light; 'he is a man in the world, but he is not of the world'. 'That man is I and I am that man.' At the dissolution of the world, when Jesus reigns over all the powers of the Light, all who receive the mystery of the Ineffable will reign with him at his right and left: 'They are I, and I am they.' The disciples' thrones will be joined to his because of what they will suffer while preaching; Mary Magdalene and John the virgin will be above all the other disciples. Those who know the word of the Ineffable will know how the world and the Height were established, and from what ground the universe arose.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'That man is I and I am that man' may mean full identity with Jesus, or only that the initiate shares his rank and kingdom; Mead's text allows both",
  "gnostic.ps.97": "Mary asks whether the word of the mystery of the Ineffable is different from the word of the whole knowledge. Jesus says yes. She asks whether one cannot inherit the kingdom without knowing the whole. He says each person who receives a mystery inherits up to the region of that mystery, but only one who knows the one word of the Ineffable has the knowledge of the universe ('I am the knowledge of the universe'), and that word can be known only after receiving the mystery of the Ineffable: 'He who receives a prophet receives a prophet's reward.' He then describes those who receive the twelve mysteries of the First Mystery: at death their souls become streams of light, pass every region without giving answers or tokens, and rule over the regions of the matching saviour; but they are not equal to those who receive the mystery of the Ineffable.",
  "gnostic.ps.98.1": "Mary asks why the First Mystery has twelve mysteries and the Ineffable only one. Jesus explains that the one mystery of the Ineffable also forms three mysteries and five mysteries, each with a different form; the five are higher than the twelve mysteries of the First Mystery, and the three higher still, but none equals the one. Whoever performs the first of them leaves the body as a stream of light. Whoever performs the second can speak it into the ears of a dying person: if that person has received mysteries, his soul too goes up as light; if not, he still escapes all judgment and fire, and is handed up to the Virgin of Light. She seals him and sends him back that same month into a righteous body that will find the godhead and the higher mysteries.",
  "gnostic.ps.98.2": "The third mystery works even for someone already in the rulers' chastisements: if its name is spoken for him, he is quickly handed up to the Virgin of Light, sealed, and reborn in a righteous body that will find the higher mystery. Jesus ranks the kingdoms: the five mysteries above the twelve of the First Mystery, the three above the five, and the one mystery of the Ineffable above all. Those who receive mysteries in the space of the Ineffable pass through without tokens or answers; so do those of the second space; but every region of the third, outer space has its receivers, answers and tokens, which he will explain later. At the dissolution of the universe he will reign for a thousand 'years of the Light' over all the powers of the Light and the perfect souls.",
  "gnostic.ps.99": "Mary asks how long a year of the Light is. Jesus says a day of the Light is a thousand years of the world, so a year of the Light is 365,000 years of the world. He describes the order of the kingdom: the disciples and those with the mystery of the Ineffable beside him; behind them those with the three mysteries, then the five, then the twelve mysteries of the First Mystery; this is the first space. Those of the second space (the First Mystery) and of the third, outer space follow, each according to the mysteries received. These are the three 'allotments' of the kingdom. The lower mysteries are written in the two Books of Yew (Jeu), which Enoch wrote while Jesus spoke with him from the tree of knowledge and the tree of life in Adam's paradise; Jesus will give the disciples the great 'head' mysteries of each allotment.",
  "gnostic.ps.100.1": "Andrew says he cannot understand how people in bodies of matter can pass through all the firmaments, rulers, gods and great powers of the Light and inherit the kingdom. Jesus, roused in spirit, rebukes him: 'How long shall I bear with you?' Humans, angels, rulers and all the great powers of the Light are all made of the same 'paste', matter and substance, out of the same Mixture. The great powers were purified by necessity, without suffering or passing from body to body. Humans are the 'refuse' left over from all of them; they suffer by being poured from one body into another, but by renouncing the world and seeking until they found the mysteries, they become purified light. That is why he came to bring the mysteries to the human race.",
  "gnostic.ps.100.2": "The powers of the Light do not need the mysteries; humans do, being material refuse: 'The healthy need no physician, but the sick.' So the disciples must preach: seek day and night until you find the purifying mysteries, and renounce the world, for buying, selling, eating and worldly cares only add more matter to the matter already in you. Because of their sufferings and repeated rebirths, and because they have received the mysteries, the disciples will rise past all the rulers, who will be ashamed before them, and all the powers will honour them. The disciples throw themselves at his feet and ask forgiveness for Andrew's 'unknowing'; Jesus says he was sent to forgive everyone's sins.",
  "gnostic.ps.subscription-1": "A heading copied at the end of a section of the manuscript: 'A portion of the books of the Saviour.' It marks the close of what Mead calls the second book.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the subscription may be a title for the whole work up to this point, or only for the part just ended",
  "gnostic.ps.101": "In a separate passage, Jesus says those worthy of the mysteries that abide in the Ineffable exist before the First Mystery; to use an image, they are like the Limbs of the Ineffable, each with its own dignity, as head, eye and ear in one body. He speaks in likenesses, not in their true form. Of all these he is the treasury, and there is no other. Blessed is anyone who finds the words of the mysteries of the outer space; one who finds those of the middle space is a god; one who finds those of the inner space is a saviour; and one who truly finds the words of the Limbs of the Ineffable is like the First himself. This is 'the knowledge of the knowledge of the Ineffable'.",
  "gnostic.ps.102.1": "Jesus tells the disciples that when he has gone into the Light they must preach to the whole world: seek day and night until you find the mysteries of the Light. Renounce the world and its cares and sins. He then gives a long list of things to renounce, each paired with the punishment it avoids: murmuring, eavesdropping, slander, false witness, pride, gluttony, babbling, craftiness, greed, love of the world, robbery, wickedness, anger, cursing, theft, fighting, ignorance, sloth and adultery, escaping the fires and rivers of the dog-faced one, Ariel, Amente, the dragon-faced ones, the lion-faced one and Yaldabaoth.",
  "gnostic.ps.102.2": "The list continues: renounce murder (to escape the crocodile-faced ruler in the cold, the first chamber of the outer darkness), impiety, atheism, magic potions, blasphemy and false teaching (the great dragon of the outer darkness). False teachers who do not repent, and those who abandon the true teaching of the First Mystery, will be frozen in the outer darkness and cease to exist at the end. Instead, people should be calm, kind, gentle, peaceful and merciful, give alms, care for the poor and sick, love God, be righteous and good, and renounce everything. To such people, even great sinners who repent, the disciples must give the mysteries without holding back: Jesus brought them so that all sins might be forgiven. 'I came not to call the righteous.'",
  "gnostic.ps.103": "Mary asks whether a perfectly righteous person who has never sinned, but has not received the mysteries, will be punished or enter the kingdom. Jesus says that at death the receivers of one of the great triple-powers take that soul, carry it round the world for three days and then lead it through the chaos and the judgments; the fires there trouble it only slightly and briefly. They quickly take pity on it and lead it up past the rulers, including the pitiless Yachthanabas, and bring it on the way of the sun to the Virgin of Light. She finds it free of sin, but cannot let it into the Light, since it lacks the sign of the kingdom of the mysteries. She seals it and sends it into a righteous body that will find the mysteries. Without the mysteries no soul, however righteous, can enter the Light.",
  "gnostic.ps.104": "John asks: if a great sinner renounces the world and is given the first mysteries, then falls back, repents, is given more, and falls again, should they forgive him up to seven times and give him the mysteries each time? Jesus says: forgive him not seven times but many times seven, and give him the mysteries of the first, outer space each time; perhaps they will win his soul. That was the meaning of 'not seven times, but seventy times seven.' Whoever saves even one soul will receive an additional honour in the Light-kingdom for each soul saved.",
  "gnostic.ps.105": "John asks whether a brother who repeatedly fails after receiving the first-space mysteries may be moved on to those of the second space. Jesus says yes, if he is sincere and failed only because of the compulsion of the Fate: give him the first mystery of the second space, then the second, then all three together. If he sins after that, they must not forgive him again; he is to be treated as a 'stumbling-block'. The three mysteries are witnesses to his last repentance, and his soul will go to the dragon of the outer darkness. This explains the saying about rebuking a brother alone, then with another, then before 'the assembly', and about 'two or three witnesses'. After that, only the first mystery of the First Mystery and the mysteries of the Ineffable can still forgive him.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the elements of the Fate' may mean the astral powers that govern a person's life, or more generally the material constitution that inclines a person to sin",
  "gnostic.ps.106": "John asks whether a very sinful but sincerely repentant brother who is found worthy may be given the mysteries of the second or third space before those of the Inheritance. Jesus says yes: if he has truly renounced the world and is not pretending or merely curious, give him whatever he is worthy of; withholding it would bring great condemnation. If he sins again, they may give them a second and a third time, but no more; the three mysteries witness his last repentance. His soul will not return to the world but will dwell in the jaws of the dragon of the outer darkness, and at the end will freeze, perish and cease to exist, unless the mystery of the First Mystery or of the Ineffable forgives him.",
  "gnostic.ps.107": "John asks what happens to people who welcome the disciples in a town with pretended devotion, receive the mysteries only to test them, and make a show of them. Jesus says: give a mystery to any household that receives you; if they prove false, invoke the first mystery of the First Mystery and ask it to take back the mysteries and make them strangers to the kingdom for ever, and shake the dust off your feet against them. All the mysteries given will return to the disciples. This is the meaning of 'if they are not worthy, let your peace return to you.' Such people will not be reborn into the world but will dwell in the dragon's jaws; only the mystery of the Ineffable can still forgive them.",
  "gnostic.ps.108": "Mary asks what a brother who has received all the mysteries can do for a relative or anyone else, sinner or not, who has died and is suffering in the judgments. Jesus says: perform the third mystery of the Ineffable and say, 'Carry the soul of this person out of all the rulers' punishments and bring him quickly to the Virgin of Light; let her seal him and place him in a righteous body.' Then all the servants of the judgments will hand the soul on until it reaches the Virgin of Light, who seals it with the sign of the kingdom of the Ineffable and has it born into a righteous body that will find the mysteries and inherit the kingdom.",
  "gnostic.ps.109": "Mary asks whether the mysteries allow a person to escape the violent death that the rulers of the Fate have appointed, by the sword, by drowning or by torture, and to die suddenly without suffering, since many persecute the disciples for Jesus's name. Jesus says that anyone who has fully performed the first mystery of the First Mystery of the Ineffable in all its forms can, whenever he speaks its name, escape what the Fate has appointed: he leaves the body at once, his soul becomes a great light-stream and rises through every region to its kingdom without giving answers anywhere.",
  "gnostic.ps.110": "Mary asks whether Jesus also brought mysteries for poverty and wealth, weakness and strength, and healing, so that unbelievers might see the disciples' power and believe. Jesus says that whoever performs the mystery of raising the dead, which he gave them earlier, and which heals demons, blindness, lameness, dumbness, deafness and every sickness, will quickly obtain whatever he asks for. The disciples cry out that his words have driven them into a frenzy: their souls, which come from him, are straining to leave their bodies and go up to his kingdom.",
  "gnostic.ps.111.1": "Jesus tells them to preach: seek until you find the mysteries of the Light, and beware of false teachings, for many will come in his name. They must give the mysteries freely to all who are worthy. But the mystery of raising the dead and healing the sick belongs to the rulers; they must not give or teach it, only use it to win belief where people reject them. Then Jesus answers Mary's earlier question, 'Who compels a person to sin?' A newborn child's power, soul and 'counterfeiting spirit' are all weak and unaware. As the child eats, each draws its own share from food: the power seeks the light of the height, the soul seeks the mixed region of righteousness, and the counterfeiting spirit seeks every evil and lust. Servitors are assigned to witness its sins.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'counterfeiting spirit' (Greek antimimon pneuma) may be understood as an external power attached to the soul, or as the soul's own lower nature; Mead's text treats it as a distinct being bound to the soul by the rulers",
  "gnostic.ps.111.2": "The power within moves the soul towards the Light, but the counterfeiting spirit drags it into all the sins the rulers of the Fate have prescribed, even stirring lusts in its dreams; it is the soul's enemy, and it is what compels a person to sin. At death the destiny leads the person to death, the receivers take the soul round the aeons for three days, and the power returns to the Virgin of Light. The soul is taken down to Amente in the chaos to be punished, with the counterfeiting spirit as its jailer. Then it is led past the rulers of the midst, punished again, and brought before the Virgin of Light as judge. She gives it a light-power and has it born into a body suited to its sins. It will not escape the cycle of bodies until it has served its last term.",
  "gnostic.ps.112.1": "A soul that resisted the counterfeiting spirit and received the mysteries of the second or third space speaks, as it rises, the mystery that undoes the seals and bonds binding the counterfeiting spirit to it. It sends the destiny back to the rulers of the midst and the counterfeiting spirit back to the rulers of the Fate, becomes a great light-stream, 'entirely wings of light', and passes through every region to its own kingdom. A soul that received the outer mysteries but later sinned also undoes the bonds, but is less free; its own receivers of the Light come and snatch it from the receivers of punishment.",
  "gnostic.ps.112.2": "The receivers of the Light become wings and garments of light for that soul. They do not take it into the chaos, which is unlawful for souls with mysteries, but along the way of the rulers of the midst. Those rulers come to meet it in terror; it speaks their 'apology' (the formula owed them), gives back its destiny and says, 'I will not come to your regions again.' It gives up the counterfeiting spirit to the rulers of the Fate in the same way, passes through the aeons above giving each region its seal and apology, and reaches the Virgin of Light and the seven virgins, who seal, baptize and anoint it. It then passes to Sabaoth the Good at the gate of Life, to Melchisedec, and into the Treasury of the Light and the Inheritance.",
  "gnostic.ps.113": "Mary says four thoughts have come to her, each linking his teaching to an earlier saying. First, the coin made of silver and copper: 'Give to the king what is the king's, and to God what is God's' means the soul gives its apology to the rulers and its glory to the Light; the silver is the power of the Light in the soul, the copper the counterfeiting spirit. Second, Paul's words 'Pay to all their dues, tax, fear, tribute, honour' mean the soul gives each region what is owed it. Third, 'A man's foes are those of his own house' are the counterfeiting spirit and the destiny. Fourth, 'Agree with your adversary while you are on the way with him, lest he hand you to the judge ... until you have paid the last farthing' describes the soul handed by the counterfeiting spirit to the Virgin of Light and sent back into bodies until its last cycle is complete.",
  "gnostic.ps.114": "Jesus praises Mary as 'all-blessed' and 'spiritual'. She says she will keep questioning so that the disciples know how to preach to all people. Jesus promises to reveal not only what they ask but things they have not thought to ask, which have not entered the human heart and which even the gods below humans do not know.",
  "gnostic.ps.115": "Mary asks how baptisms forgive sins, if the retributive servitors witness every sin. Jesus explains that the servitors convict only souls without mysteries and hold them in the chaos; they cannot reach souls that have received mysteries. While a person sins, the servitors witness it and the counterfeiting spirit fixes each sin on the soul as a seal, so that the rulers can count them. But the mystery of baptism becomes a great, wise fire that enters the soul secretly and burns away all the sins the counterfeiting spirit has sealed on it. It then separates the counterfeiting spirit, the destiny and the body into one portion, and the soul and the power into another, and stays between them to keep the soul clean of matter.",
  "gnostic.ps.116": "Mary links baptism to earlier sayings of Jesus: 'I am come to cast fire on the earth', 'I have a baptism to be baptized with, and how shall I endure until it is accomplished?', and 'I came not to bring peace but division; five in one house, three against two and two against three.' The fire is the mysteries of baptism, which burn away the soul's sins. He will not stay in the world until the baptisms have purified the perfect souls. The division is baptism separating the counterfeiting spirit, the body and the destiny (three) from the soul and the power (two). Jesus praises her as 'spiritual and light-pure'.",
  "gnostic.ps.117": "Mary asks whether the mysteries of the three spaces, of the First Mystery and of the Ineffable forgive sins in the same way as the baptisms. Jesus says the mysteries of the three spaces forgive all the sins the soul has committed, in every region of the rulers, and also the sins it will commit, for as long as each mystery remains effective. The mystery of the First Mystery and the mysteries of the Ineffable forgive all sins and, beyond that, never count any sin against the soul again, for all eternity.",
  "gnostic.ps.118": "Mary matches this to David: 'Blessed are they whose iniquities are forgiven and whose sins are covered' (the three spaces), and 'Blessed is the man to whom the Lord will not impute sin' (the First Mystery and the Ineffable). She then asks whether someone who received the mysteries of the First Mystery, sinned and repented can be forgiven by praying in his mystery. Jesus says such a person can be forgiven up to twelve times; after that he cannot, unless he receives the mysteries of the Ineffable, which forgive at every time.",
  "gnostic.ps.119": "Mary asks about those with the mysteries of the First Mystery who sin and die without repenting. Jesus says that their judgment is harsher than any other: they dwell in the dragon of the outer darkness and in the end freeze and perish, because they received the gift and did not keep it. Those who received the mysteries of the Ineffable are forgiven every time they sincerely repent while still alive. But if they sin and die unrepentant, their judgment is the harshest of all, even if their souls are new to the world: they will never return to bodies, but will be cast into the outer darkness and cease to exist for ever.",
  "gnostic.ps.120": "Mary compares this to the saying about salt: if salt loses its savour, it is fit neither for the soil nor for the dunghill, and is thrown away. Those who received the mysteries of the Ineffable and then fall away are likewise fit for nothing and are cast into the outer darkness. Mary asks how often those who sin only through the compulsion of the Fate and sincerely repent will be forgiven. Jesus says: every time, while they live, because those mysteries are compassionate. But if they die without repenting, they will perish in the dragon like the others. That is why, if people knew the hour of their death, they would guard themselves and not sin.",
  "gnostic.ps.121": "Mary recalls the saying: 'If the householder knew at what hour the thief would come, he would stay awake.' Jesus tells them to warn all who receive the mysteries to watch themselves and not sin, lest they die unrepentant. Mary marvels at the great compassion of the mysteries. Jesus answers: if an earthly king can pardon murderers and other capital crimes, or a soldier wearing the king's robe cannot be punished for what he does abroad, how much more can the Ineffable and the First Mystery, lords of all, forgive those who wear the vestures of their mysteries.",
  "gnostic.ps.122": "A woman comes to repent whom Jesus had baptized three times without her living up to it. To test Peter's mercy, Jesus tells him to perform the mystery that cuts a soul off from the Inheritance of the Light. Peter asks him to let her have one more chance with the higher mysteries, and to cut her off only if she still fails. Jesus sees that Peter is as compassionate as he is. Mary explains this from the parable of the fig tree that bore no fruit for three years: the gardener asked to spare it one more year and dig and manure it, and cut it down only if it still bore nothing.",
  "gnostic.ps.123": "Mary asks whether a person who received mysteries, sinned and then truly repented may be given the same mystery again, or a lower one. Jesus says neither the same nor a lower mystery can forgive him, only a higher one; so the disciples should give him a higher mystery. But if he has received the three mysteries of the two spaces or the third, and then sinned, no mystery will help except the mystery of the First Mystery and those of the Ineffable. Someone who has received mysteries of the second or third space and has not sinned may go on to receive any mysteries he wishes, from first to last.",
  "gnostic.ps.124": "Mary asks who will suffer more after death: someone who knew the godhead and received the mysteries but sinned and did not repent, or an ignorant, impious sinner. Jesus says the one who knew will suffer far more. Mary explains from the saying that the servant who knew his master's will and did not do it will be beaten with many blows, and the one who did not know with few: 'to whom much is given, of him much will be required.'",
  "gnostic.ps.125": "Mary asks whether souls that put off receiving the mysteries, expecting to get them in a later life, are in danger of missing them. Jesus tells the disciples to warn everyone not to delay. When the number of perfect souls is complete, he will shut the gates of the Light, and no one will enter or leave. Before he sets fire to the world to purify it, the faith and mysteries will be revealed even more, and some reborn souls will find the mysteries too late: they will knock, saying 'Lord, open to us', and he will say 'I do not know you', and send them into the outer darkness. Mary begins to explain, but here the manuscript has a large gap.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'I will now shut the gates' may describe a future event at the end of time, or warn that the moment could come at any time; the text says only that people do not know when",
  "gnostic.ps.126": "Mary asks what the outer darkness is like. Jesus says it is a great dragon with its tail in its mouth, lying outside the whole world and encircling it. Inside are twelve great dungeons of punishment, each with a ruler of a different face: a crocodile (Enchthonin, from whose jaws come ice, dust, cold and disease), a cat (Charachar), a dog (Archaroch), a serpent (Achrochar), a black bull (Marchur), a wild boar (Lamchamor), a bear (Luchar), a vulture (Laraoch) and a basilisk (Archeoch); the tenth, eleventh and twelfth hold many rulers with seven dragon, cat or dog heads, led by Xarmaroch, Rochar and Chremaor. Their names and faces change every hour. Each dungeon has a door opening upwards, guarded by an angel set by Yew, the First Man, so that the dragon and its rulers cannot rebel.",
  "gnostic.ps.127": "Souls are not led into the dragon through these doors. Unrepentant blasphemers, false teachers, men who have intercourse with males, the impure, atheists, murderers, adulterers and sorcerers, and souls that have used up their appointed cycles of rebirth without repenting, are drawn in through the jaws of its tail; then the dragon puts its tail back in its mouth and shuts them in. Each door bears one of the dragon's twelve true names, which are all contained in one another. Its punishments are worse than any other: the souls freeze in cold, hail and fire, and at the end perish for ever. The fire of Amente is nine times hotter than earthly fire, the chaos nine times hotter than Amente, the rulers of the midst nine times hotter again, and the dragon seventy times hotter still.",
  "gnostic.ps.128": "Mary and the disciples weep for sinners. Mary asks what a good person who has all the mysteries can do for an unrepentant relative bound for the outer darkness. Jesus says: perform the mystery of the Ineffable that forgives sins at every time, and then say that the soul you are thinking of should be removed from every punishment and led to the Virgin of Light to be sealed and reborn into a righteous body; or, if it has completed its cycles, led to the seven virgins of the Light to be baptized and sealed. Then the dragon will take its tail out of its mouth and release the soul, Melchisedec's receivers will carry it to the Virgin of Light, and it will be reborn, or taken into the Treasury of the Light to wait in its last order.",
  "gnostic.ps.129": "Mary recalls that those who receive the mysteries of the Ineffable or the First Mystery become flames of light-beams and light-streams that pass through all regions. Jesus says that this applies to those who received the mystery while alive. Sinners who died unrepentant and for whom others perform the mystery of the Ineffable cannot pass through the regions on their own, because they did not perform the mystery themselves; instead Melchisedec's receivers and the rulers' servants hand them on until they reach the Virgin of Light.",
  "gnostic.ps.130.1": "Mary pleads for someone who received the mysteries of the first space but grew careless: he did not say the prayer that removes the evil from food and drink, was bound again to the Fate, sinned, and died before he could repent and receive higher mysteries. He has been taken into the dragon of the outer darkness and has no one in the world to perform the mystery of the Ineffable for him. She asks Jesus, 'the key that opens and shuts the door of the universe', to have mercy on such people, who believed in his mysteries sincerely, if only for a single day. Jesus calls her most blessed, and says that while such people are still alive they should be given the mystery of one of the twelve names of the dungeons of the dragon.",
  "gnostic.ps.130.2": "Anyone who knows one of those twelve names, even a great sinner, can speak it while being punished inside the dragon; the dragon will be shaken, the door of that dungeon will open upwards, and its ruler will throw the soul out. The angels of Yew, the First Man, will take it to Yew. If it still has rebirths left, his receivers keep it until someone performs the mystery of the Ineffable for it, so it can be reborn into a good body. If it has none left and lacks the sign of the Ineffable, Yew has it baptized (but not anointed) by the seven virgins of the Light and placed apart in the Treasury until the end. Mary explains this from the saying 'Make friends for yourselves with the mammon of unrighteousness, so that they may receive you into the eternal tents': the mammon is the dragon of the outer darkness.",
  "gnostic.ps.131.1": "Mary asks whether the dragon comes into this world. Jesus says that when the sun is above the world it covers the dragon's darkness; when the sun is below, the dragon's darkness veils it and its breath enters the world as night-smoke; if it came in its true form the world would be destroyed. Mary asks who compels people to sin. Jesus says the rulers of the Fate, though not by coming down in person. When an old soul is about to be born, the rulers at the head of the aeons, in the kingdom of Adamas, give it a cup of forgetfulness mixed from the seed of wickedness, and it forgets everything it has passed through. That cup becomes a copy of the soul outside it, the counterfeiting spirit. New souls are kneaded by the five great rulers of the Fate from the sweat, tears and breath of the rulers, or from the refuse of the purification of the Light, and they too receive the cup.",
  "gnostic.ps.131.2": "The five great rulers of the Fate and the rulers of the disks of the sun and moon breathe into the soul, and with their breath comes a portion of Jesus's own power, which the last Helper cast into the Mixture. That power stays inside the soul, free and self-governing, giving it the sense to seek the Light of the Height; it keeps the soul upright. The counterfeiting spirit is placed outside the soul and bound to it with the rulers' seals, so that it keeps driving the soul to sin and keeps it their slave through rebirth after rebirth. Jesus promises to explain later how souls of humans, birds, beasts and reptiles are formed. He brought the mysteries to undo these bonds and free the soul from its 'parents', the rulers; that is why he said: 'Whoever does not leave father and mother to follow me is not worthy of me.'",
  "gnostic.ps.132.1": "Salome objects that the Law of Moses says 'Whoever abandons father and mother shall die.' Mary asks to answer her: the Law was not speaking of the soul, the body or the counterfeiting spirit, which are children of the rulers, but of the power that came from the Saviour, the 'light-man' within us; whoever stays apart from the Saviour and his mysteries, our true parents, will not only die but be destroyed. Salome embraces her. Jesus then explains further who compels people to sin: the rulers bind the counterfeiting spirit to the soul and order it to drive the soul to sin and, after death, to drag it through every judgment for those sins, unless the soul speaks the mysteries that undo its bonds and seals. Such a soul belongs to the Light and must be let go; otherwise it is punished and sent back into the cycle of bodies.",
  "gnostic.ps.132.2": "The rulers hand the bound soul and counterfeiting spirit to their 365 servitors, with the power inside the soul to keep both upright. The servitors bring them down into the world, where the rulers of the midst watch over them, together with the destiny (Moira), which will lead the person to the death the Fate has appointed. They divide all these into two portions and place one in a man and one in a woman through food, air or drink, and draw the two together however far apart they live. The 365 servitors then enter the woman's womb, hold back the blood of her food for forty days and knead it, spend thirty more days building the limbs in the image of a human body, and then summon into it the counterfeiting spirit, the soul and the power, with the destiny following outside.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 365 servitors may correspond to the days of the year, linking the building of the body to the calendar; the text does not say so",
  "gnostic.ps.132.3": "The servitors seal the body with the dates of each stage: the day they entered the womb on the left hand, the day the body was finished on the right, others on different parts of the skull, the day the power was breathed in on the brain and heart, and the number of years the soul will live on the forehead. They report all these seals to the rulers of punishment, so that their receivers know when to take the soul out and can send servitors to witness its sins. At birth the power, soul and counterfeiting spirit are small, but the destiny is large, since it is not mixed into the body; it follows the person until it brings about the appointed death, by wild beast, serpent, a fall, hanging, drowning or any other way. That is its only task. 'If you could understand it, I am that mystery.'",
  "gnostic.ps.133": "Mary asks whether everything appointed by the Fate, good or bad, sin, death or life, must happen to everyone. Jesus says yes, everything appointed comes to pass. That is why he brought the keys of the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven: without them no one, righteous or sinner, could be saved. He frees those who believe from the bonds and seals of the rulers' aeons and binds them to the seals and orders of the Light, so that what he frees or binds on earth is freed or bound above. He proclaimed this openly to all: 'Seek and ye shall find, knock and it shall be opened.' John the Baptist foretold him: 'He that cometh after me ... his fan is in his hand; he will burn the chaff and gather the wheat.'",
  "gnostic.ps.134": "Mary asks how people who meet false teachings can tell whether they come from Jesus. He says: 'Be like skilful money-changers; keep the good and throw out the bad.' Just as people read the weather from the winds, they should test any teaching by whether it agrees with his words, confirmed by two or three witnesses, and with the order of the air, heavens, stars, earth and waters. Even the righteous need the mysteries contained in the Books of Yew, which he had Enoch write in Paradise and hide in the rock of Ararad, guarded from the flood and the rulers by the ruler Kalapatauroth. Mary says no one in the world can be free of every sin. Jesus agrees: one in a thousand and two in ten thousand will accomplish the mystery of the First Mystery; all are under sin and need the mysteries.",
  "gnostic.ps.135": "Mary asks whether any soul entered the Light before Jesus came. He says none did; he has opened the gates and the ways. Not even the prophets entered: the rulers spoke with them and gave them the mystery of the aeons. When he came, he sent Elijah into the body of John the Baptist and placed the other prophets in righteous bodies that will find the mysteries. He forgave Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, gave them the mysteries in the aeons and placed them with Yabraoth and the repentant rulers; he will take them into the Light, but only after Mary and the disciples. The other patriarchs and righteous people since Adam he has had reborn, through the Virgin of Light, in righteous bodies. Mary says they now know clearly that he brought the keys of the mysteries.",
  "gnostic.ps.subscription-2": "A second heading copied at the end of a section of the manuscript: 'A portion of the books of the Saviour.' It closes what Mead treats as the fourth book, before the different material that follows.",
  "gnostic.ps.136": "After the crucifixion and resurrection, the disciples gather round Jesus. Standing at an altar on the water of the Ocean, with the disciples placed at the four quarters, he prays to 'my Father, father of all fatherhood, boundless Light', in a string of sacred names and vowel sounds, and explains 'iao': iota, the universe has gone forth; alpha, it will turn back; omega, all will be completed. The heavens, aeons and sphere move away to the west. The disk of the sun appears as a great dragon with its tail in its mouth, drawn by four white horses; the moon as a ship steered by two dragons and drawn by two white bulls. Jesus and the disciples are left in the air on the 'way of the midst' below the sphere. He tells how Yew bound the rebellious rulers of Adamas into the sphere, rescued the repentant Yabraoth, and set over the bound rulers five great regents whom people call Kronos, Ares, Hermes, Aphrodite and Zeus.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the five regents correspond to the planets Saturn, Mars, Mercury, Venus and Jupiter; Mead's text gives only the Greek god-names",
  "gnostic.ps.137": "Yew bound a power into each regent: from the great Invisible into Kronos, from two of the triple-powered gods into Ares and Hermes, and from Pistis Sophia, daughter of Barbelo, into Aphrodite. Seeing that the world needed a good steersman, he took a power from the little Sabaoth, the Good, and bound it into Zeus, so that Zeus would spend thirteen months in each aeon freeing the rulers he visits from their wickedness, and gave him two aeons opposite those of Hermes as his home. Jesus also gives their incorruptible names: Orimouth (Kronos), Mounichounaphor (Ares), Tarpetanouph (Hermes), Chosi (Aphrodite) and Chonbal (Zeus).\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'thirteen months' may be an error for 'three months', as Mead's bracket suggests; a stay of roughly a year in each sign would fit Jupiter's twelve-year cycle",
  "gnostic.ps.138": "The disciples bow down and ask what these ways of the midst are. Mary kisses his feet and asks what they are for, how souls are seized and punished there, how long the punishments last, and how they can escape them. Weeping, she asks him to save them. Jesus promises to give them all the mysteries and knowledge: the mysteries, seals, ciphers and invocations of the twelve aeons, the thirteenth aeon, the baptism of those of the Midst and of the Right, and the great mystery of the Treasury of the Light, so that they will be called 'children of the fulness'.",
  "gnostic.ps.139": "Jesus says Yew also bound 360 rulers of Adamas into the air below the sphere, under five great rulers of the way of the midst. The first is Paraplex, a female ruler with hair to her feet, whose twenty-five archdemons enter people and drive them to rage, cursing and slander, then carry off their souls. Yew and Zorokothora Melchisedec periodically go down to purify the light in the rulers; when they withdraw, the rulers rise up in anger and seize what souls they can. Paraplex torments the souls of the passionate, the cursers and slanderers in dark smoke and fire for 133 years and nine months, until Zeus and Aphrodite reach certain signs (the Ram and the Balance); then the great Sabaoth looks down from the Right, her regions are dissolved, and the souls are cast back into the sphere.",
  "gnostic.ps.140.1": "The second ruler of the way of the midst is Ariouth the Ethiopian, a wholly black female ruler, whose fourteen demons stir up quarrels, wars and murders; she torments the souls she seizes for 113 years, until Zeus is in the Crab and Aphrodite in the Goat, when Yew looks out and her regions are dissolved. The third is triple-faced Hekate, whose twenty-seven demons lead people to perjury, lies and covetousness; her captives suffer 105 years and six months, until Zeus is in the Scorpion and Aphrodite in the Bull, when Zorokothora Melchisedec looks out and her regions are destroyed. Each time the souls are thrown back into the sphere.",
  "gnostic.ps.140.2": "The fourth is Parhedron Typhon, whose thirty-two demons lead people to lust, fornication and adultery; his captives suffer 128 years, until Zeus is in the Archer and Aphrodite in the Twins, when Zarazaz (called 'Maskelli' by the rulers) looks out and dissolves his regions. The fifth is Yachthanabas, whose demons make people show favouritism, take bribes, pervert justice and forget the poor, and fill their souls with forgetfulness; his captives suffer 150 years and eight months, until Zeus is in the Water-carrier and Aphrodite in the Lion, when the great Iao, the Good, looks out and destroys his regions. These, Jesus says, are the workings of the ways of the midst.",
  "gnostic.ps.141": "The disciples beg mercy for themselves and all humankind, groping like the blind in darkness. Jesus comforts them: he will make them lords over all these powers and give them the keys of the kingdom of heaven. He sings a hymn in the great name; the ways of the midst vanish, and they stand in a very bright light. He breathes into their eyes; they see a great light, and then fire, water, wine and blood. Jesus explains that these are all he brought into the world: water and fire from the Treasury of the Light, wine and blood from Barbelo; the fire, water and wine purify sins, and the blood was a sign of the body he received. He links them to his sayings about casting fire on the earth, living water, the cup of the covenant, and the water and blood from his side. Back on the Mount of Galilee, he promises to give them the mystery of the forgiveness of sins.",
  "gnostic.ps.142": "Jesus asks for fire and vine branches and sets out an offering: two wine-vessels, cups of water and wine, and loaves, one for each disciple. With the disciples behind him in linen, holding the cipher of the name of the father of the Treasury of the Light, he prays to the Father in sacred names and calls on the 'forgivers of sins' to forgive the disciples' sins, known and unknown, including fornication and adultery, and to make them worthy of his Father's kingdom; he asks for a sign in the offering. The sign appears, and he tells them their sins are forgiven. This rite, 'the baptism of the first offering', they must perform only for sincere believers who keep his commandments, and keep hidden from everyone else.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the 'sign in the offering' is not described; it may be a change in the elements (for example water turning to wine) or some other visible token",
  "gnostic.ps.143": "The disciples ask for the mysteries of the fire-baptism, the baptism of the holy spirit of the Light and the spiritual anointing, which lead souls into the Treasury of the Light. Jesus says that nothing is higher than these, except the mystery of the seven Voices with their forty-nine powers, and the one name that contains all names, lights and powers. A soul that knows that name cannot be held back by any darkness, ruler or power after death: spoken to the fire it quenches it; spoken to the demons and receivers of the outer darkness it makes them sink down crying 'Holy, holy'; spoken to Barbelo, the invisible god and the triple-powered gods it makes them collapse and beg the Light to remember them. The disciples weep aloud, and here eight leaves of the manuscript are missing.",
  "gnostic.ps.144": "The text resumes in the middle of the punishment of the curser: after the fire-rivers and the rulers of the way of the midst, the soul is judged by the Virgin of Light and cast into the sphere, where a water below it turns to seething fire and purifies it. Then Yaluham, the receiver of Sabaoth the Adamas, gives it the cup of forgetfulness, and it is born into a body whose heart is always troubled. Mary asks about the persistent slanderer: Ariel's receivers lead his soul round the world for three days, then he is punished for eleven months and twenty-one days in Amente by Ariel, again by Yaldabaoth and his forty-nine demons, again in the fire-rivers and fire-seas, and again on the way of the midst; then he is purified, given the cup of forgetfulness, and reborn into a body that will always be afflicted.",
  "gnostic.ps.145": "Mary cries, 'Woe to sinners!' Salome asks about a murderer whose only sin is murder. Jesus says Yaldabaoth's receivers bind his soul by the feet to a great horse-faced demon, who carries it round the world for three days. Then it is punished for three years and six months in the region of cold and snow, as long again by Yaldabaoth's forty-nine demons, as long by Persephone in the chaos, and as long by the rulers of the way of the midst. The Virgin of Light then orders it cast into the outer darkness until the darkness of the midst is taken up, when it will be destroyed and dissolved.",
  "gnostic.ps.146": "Peter asks that the women stop questioning so that the men can ask too, and Jesus tells Mary and the women to give their brothers a turn. Peter asks about the habitual thief: Adonis's receivers take his soul; it is punished three months, eight days and two hours each by Ariel in Amente, by Yaldabaoth's demons and by the rulers of the midst, then purified, given the cup of forgetfulness, and reborn lame, crippled and blind. Andrew asks about the arrogant: twenty months each with Ariel, Yaldabaoth and the rulers of the midst, then rebirth in a lame, deformed body that everyone despises. Thomas asks about the persistent blasphemer: bound by the tongue to a horse-faced demon, punished eleven years in the cold and snow and eleven with Yaldabaoth, then cast into the outer darkness until the great dragon-faced ruler is judged, when the soul freezes and is destroyed.",
  "gnostic.ps.147": "Bartholomew asks about a man who has intercourse with a male; Jesus says both partners suffer as the blasphemer does: eleven years with Yaldabaoth, eleven in fire-rivers and pitch-seas full of pig-faced demons, then the outer darkness and destruction. Thomas reports people who eat a porridge made of semen and menstrual blood, saying 'We believe in Esau and Jacob.' Jesus, angry, says this is worse than any sin: they go straight to the outer darkness and are destroyed. John asks about a good person who never found the mysteries. Jesus says the receivers of Bainchoooch lead that soul gently, show it the punishments without inflicting them, and bring it to the Virgin of Light. After purification and the cup of forgetfulness, a receiver of the little Sabaoth gives it a cup of wisdom and soberness, so that in its next body it cannot rest until it finds the mysteries of the Light.",
  "gnostic.ps.148": "Mary asks whether someone who has committed every sin without finding the mysteries is punished for each sin separately; Jesus says yes, for every one. John asks whether such a person can be saved if he finally finds the mysteries; Jesus says yes, if he performs them and sins no more, he will inherit the Treasury of the Light. Jesus adds that souls sent into the world when Zeus and Aphrodite stand before the Virgin of Light, with Kronos and Ares behind her, will be good and find the mysteries; souls sent when Ares and Kronos stand before her will be wicked and will not find them. Hearing this in the midst of Amente, the disciples weep for sinners and beg him, 'son of the Holy One', to have mercy on them, for they too have sinned.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- the positions of Zeus, Aphrodite, Kronos and Ares 'before' and 'behind' the Virgin may refer to the planets Jupiter, Venus, Saturn and Mars in relation to the constellation Virgo at the time of birth; the text does not spell out the astronomy",
  "gnostic.ps.postscript": "A short ending added on the last page of the manuscript, perhaps from another work. The apostles went out three by three to the four quarters of the world and preached the good news of the kingdom, with Christ working with them through words, signs and wonders, so that the kingdom of God became known throughout the earth and all Israel, as a witness to all nations from east to west. The last two lines are erased.",
};
